## Intro 1 — Why vulnerability discovery still doesn't scale

- Fuzzers run continuously at ecosystem scale, but their output is crashes, not confirmed vulnerabilities
- Every crash still needs a human to answer: is it reachable, is it a real bug, what class, how severe
- That hand-off is where throughput dies: triage takes expert hours per crash, and most crashes are noise
- Writing the harness that makes a codebase fuzzable at all is still a manual, expert task
- The question behind this work: can one system own discovery end to end — harness, fuzz, confirm, report — unattended

*Notes: one message — the bottleneck has moved from running fuzzers to deciding what to fuzz and what the results mean.*

## Intro 2 — Cyber Reasoning Systems and the AIxCC setting

- A Cyber Reasoning System (CRS) finds vulnerabilities and proves them with a reproducing input, with no human in the loop
- DARPA's Cyber Grand Challenge (2016) did this for synthetic binaries; AIxCC (2023–2025) did it for real open-source software with LLMs allowed
- An AIxCC task is an OSS-Fuzz-style project: source, build script, provided fuzz harnesses, sometimes a diff to focus on
- Scoring counts what is proven: a proof-of-vulnerability (PoV) that reproduces on the judge's build; unproven claims and duplicates score nothing
- Hard constraints: fixed compute and LLM budget, fixed time, and every submission is judged — false positives cost

*Notes: define PoV as the scored artifact. Everything in the architecture exists to turn a crash into a submittable PoV under a budget.*

## Intro 3 — discver in one slide

- An autonomous CRS for Java, C, C++, Go, Rust and Python, running under OSS-CRS, the open framework for AIxCC-style tasks
- Finds or writes fuzz harnesses, ranks entry points, and admits a generated harness only after it proves it reaches project code
- Fuzzes with libFuzzer and Jazzer, tracks harness health, and steers budget toward the task's diff when there is one
- Deduplicates crashes and triages each one with an LLM arbiter grounded in a knowledge base of past bugs; static findings are confirmed by directed fuzzing before they count
- Submits with confidence tiers — PoV-backed findings always, model- or scanner-only findings above a threshold
- Learns from history without training: a compiled knowledge base from OSS-Fuzz vulnerabilities, ARVO and the CWE catalogue; a downstream patch stage exists and is out of scope for this talk

*Notes: the "what"; the rest of the deck is the "how". Thresholds (0.67 submit, 0.85 for arbiter-overruled leads) belong on the methodology slide.*

