## Literature 1 — Cyber Reasoning Systems: from CGC to AIxCC

- **DARPA CGC (2016):** first all-machine hacking tournament; Mayhem (ForAllSecure) won; Xandra and Shellphish's Mechanical Phish placed; hybrid fuzzing + symbolic execution (Driller, NDSS 2016) was the dominant recipe
- **DARPA AIxCC (2023–2025):** real open-source targets, LLMs allowed; ~40 teams in the semifinal, 7 finalists; final at DEF CON 33, August 2025
- **AIxCC final results:** 1st Team Atlanta (Atlantis), 2nd Trail of Bits (Buttercup), 3rd Theori (RoboDuck); across all teams 54 of 70 synthetic vulnerabilities were discovered, plus 18 real-world vulnerabilities *(re-check the totals against DARPA's announcement)*
- **Common finalist architecture for discovery:** many fuzzers in parallel (libFuzzer, AFL++, Jazzer) · LLM-generated harnesses and seeds · directed fuzzing toward the diff · LLM-assisted triage of crashes
- All finalist systems were open-sourced after the final — discver's reference set includes Atlantis, Buttercup and all-you-need-is-a-fuzzing-brain

*Notes: lineage. The finalists converged on the same discovery loop; the differences are in how harnesses are produced and how findings are confirmed before submission.*

## Literature 2 — Fuzzing infrastructure and LLM-written harnesses

- **OSS-Fuzz (Google, 2016–):** continuous fuzzing for >1,000 open-source projects; thousands of vulnerabilities found; defines the project/build/harness layout CRSs consume
- **ClusterFuzz:** the engine behind it — crash deduplication by stack signature, regression ranges, corpus management
- **Jazzer (Code Intelligence, 2021):** coverage-guided fuzzing for the JVM with sanitizers for injection, deserialization and SSRF — the Java engine in OSS-Fuzz and in discver
- **OSS-Fuzz-Gen (Google, 2024):** LLMs write fuzz targets for OSS-Fuzz projects at scale, with build-and-coverage feedback to filter bad drivers
- **Studies of LLM fuzz-driver generation:** Zhang et al., ISSTA 2024 ("How effective are they?") — LLMs produce usable drivers for most APIs but need validation; PromptFuzz (Lyu et al., CCS 2024); Fuzz4All (Xia et al., ICSE 2024); ChatAFL for protocols (Meng et al., NDSS 2024)
- **Directed fuzzing:** AFLGo (Böhme et al., CCS 2017) — steer the fuzzer toward target locations; the basis for diff-aware targeting

*Notes: two takeaways — harness quality is the main lever, and a generated harness must be validated by evidence (coverage, reach), which is what discver's gate enforces.*

## Literature 3 — Triage: deciding what a crash means

- **Deduplication is unsolved:** stack-hash signatures both merge distinct bugs and split one bug into many; Klees et al. (CCS 2018) showed fuzzing evaluations hinge on it; root-cause clustering (Igor, CCS 2021) improves but does not close the gap
- **LLM verdicts alone are unreliable:** Ullah et al. (IEEE S&P 2024) — LLMs cannot yet reliably identify and reason about vulnerabilities; PrimeVul (Ding et al., ICSE 2025) — detection accuracy collapses on realistic data
- **Grounding helps:** retrieval over known vulnerability knowledge improves LLM judgements (Vul-RAG, 2024) — the case for a knowledge base behind the arbiter
- **Confirmation beats classification:** a static or model finding becomes evidence only when a fuzzer produces an input that reaches and triggers it — directed fuzzing (AFLGo) as the confirmation step
- **Design consequence for discver:** crash signature → KB-grounded triage → arbiter with confidence → directed confirmation for static leads → tiered submission; no single model verdict decides

*Notes: this slide justifies the triage design. The thresholds and the "weak lead" tier come from the fact that model confidence is informative but not trustworthy on its own.*

## Literature 4 — Datasets and benchmarks the work draws on

- **OSV / google/oss-fuzz-vulns:** machine-readable records of OSS-Fuzz-found vulnerabilities — project, crash type, affected ranges; the primary source of the knowledge base's recipes
- **ARVO (Mei et al., 2024):** ~5,000 reproducible OSS-Fuzz vulnerabilities with working builds and PoVs — the "how was it found" layer of the KB
- **CWE (MITRE):** weakness taxonomy; discver compiles per-CWE playbooks (what to look for, how to reach it)
- **CyberGym (Wang et al., 2025):** 1,507 tasks from 188 OSS-Fuzz projects asking agents to reproduce real vulnerabilities; the best agents succeeded on roughly one task in ten *(re-check the headline rate for the model generation you cite)*
- **CRSBench:** AIxCC-derived benchmark used for our evaluation — known vulnerabilities per problem, scored on PoVs; recall and precision per trial

*Notes: ties the KB to its sources and names the yardsticks the results section uses. If asked "why not train" — these corpora are small for training but large for retrieval, which is the workshop design choice.*

